hi there its senior editor Kevin van org  standing in for reviewer Nathan Minnie a  to bring you this early access review of  Hawking stunning sci-fi battle escapes  and impressively detailed robot designs  are an attractive wrapper for the fast  and furious flavor of action in Hawken  an online free-to-play MEC shooter that  brings a new level of beauty and  excitement to the mechs onra lots of  mech games do a decent enough job of  making you feel like you're strapped  into a huge hunk of walking metal but  Hawken really nails it hopping into the  cockpit and taking your first heavy  steps into this atmospheric sci-fi realm  is a wild ride as the powerful sights  and sounds that accompany your every  move  help to draw you deeper into your role  as a badass mech pilot it's easy to get  wooed by this Pleasant sensory overload  but pausing for too long to admire your  well-armed ride and the meticulous  detailed surroundings is a fatal mistake  Hawkins firefights move at a fast clip  despite their girth and heft on the  battlefield mechs are surprisingly agile  thanks to fuel power jump jets that let  you sidestep incoming fire leap over  obstacles glide short distances and  hover in place this mobility is great  because it doesn't detract from the  impact of being a mech pilot and it  keeps matches flowing the high energy of  standard free-for-all in team deathmatch  iziz fun in its own right and excellent  for scoring experience and currency to  beef up your mech fleet that said the  grand scope of Hawkins other core modes  holds a very different kind of thrill  missile assault puts a cool spin on  point capture since every turret your  team holds control over continually  hurls rockets towards your enemy's base  siege mode ramps up the spectacle to an  even more impressive scale with longer  matches that involve launching and  annihilating stardestroyer styled  battleships that push across the skies  above the battlefield  aside from being a blast these modes do  a phenomenal job of making your  skirmishes feel like they're a critical  part of a bigger conflict Hawkins sports  some truly impressive in  of maps they sprawl out in all  directions and mix in high vantage  points loads of obstacles to use for  cover and subterranean regions to fight  through their gorgeous - and visually  diverse enough to keep match cycles  feeling fresh the diversity extends to  the wide assortment of mechs you pilot  from light and zippy to big and menacing  there's a mech for all tastes and  fighting styles different handling armor  weapon loadouts and combat roles also  leave lots of room for tactical variety  sinking off foes through the scope of a  long-range sniper mech offers a  different kind of satisfaction from  hopping into an ordnance heavy mech and  Dell using the enemy with clusters of  rockets other specialties and special  abilities are geared towards support  assaults assassination and defense to  round out your options unlocking them  unfortunately can be a slow and somewhat  tedious process if you don't want to  shell out a bit of real cash for bonus  XP boosts or instant unlock access to  speed things along almost everything you  can buy in the game ranging from totally  optional decorations to actual mechs and  weapon components can be bought with  accumulated in-game cash you accrue  through playing matches but even if you  want a little boost to ease the grind  temporary XP and money boosters are  quite cheap on the whole nothing is  outrageously expensive so it all comes  down to a matter of patience and  playtime the sheer time it takes to  build substantive progress across your  mech fleet is a muddy point in an  otherwise excellent game does that have  a huge impact on the overall quality of  the experience not really Hawken goes  far above and beyond what you'd expect  from a mech combat game with impressive  visuals and intense rewarding gameplay  to match  you
 

